KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ at offset 0: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ d follows c: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ size 8: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The expected output is that of g++ 13 and clang++ 18.  CBMC places the
member at offset 0 too, but keeps it in the sequence of the components as
a zero-length array of A8 (make_empty_potentially_overlapping_members_
zero_sized), which add_padding aligns to 8: d moves to offset 8 and
sizeof(Q) is 16.  The program fails the second and third assertions.

Why FUTURE and not KNOWNBUG: the layout conforms to N5008 (the size and
the offsets of members are implementation-defined, [intro.object]/8,
[class.mem.general]); only GCC's and Clang's Itanium C++ ABI layout is not
matched.

What it would take: give the zero-length array component an alignment of
1 (so that it does not pad the sequence) while the class keeps the
member's alignment, e.g. by recording it as the class's ID_C_alignment;
or not keep the member among the sequential components at all.
